# =============================================================================
# Ledger — local environment
# Filled-in example for a Supabase-hosted Postgres. Replace every bracketed
# placeholder with your own project's values.
# Save this file as `.env` in the repo root.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TWO urls, on purpose:
#   DATABASE_URL -> the transaction POOLER (port 6543). What the running app uses.
#   DIRECT_URL   -> a DIRECT session connection (port 5432). What migrations use.
# Migrations need prepared statements and advisory locks, which PgBouncer in
# transaction mode does not support — run them through the pooler and
# `prisma migrate` fails in confusing ways. See prisma.config.ts.
#
# >>> REPLACE [YOUR-DB-PASSWORD] IN BOTH LINES <<<
# Supabase dashboard -> Project Settings -> Database -> Database password
# ("Reset database password" if you never saved it). This is NOT the
# publishable/anon key — that key cannot open a Postgres connection.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.[YOUR-PROJECT-REF]:[YOUR-DB-PASSWORD]@aws-0-[YOUR-REGION].pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres?pgbouncer=true&connection_limit=5&sslmode=require"
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://postgres.[YOUR-PROJECT-REF]:[YOUR-DB-PASSWORD]@aws-0-[YOUR-REGION].pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres?sslmode=require"

# ON sslmode=require:
# node-postgres does not follow libpq semantics here — it treats `require` as
# `verify-full`, i.e. full certificate-chain verification. That is what you
# want, and it is what these URLs ask for.
#
# It can fail with "self-signed certificate in certificate chain", either
# because the pooler's CA is not in the local trust store or because a
# corporate TLS proxy is re-signing the connection. Two ways out, in order of
# preference:
#   - Download Supabase's CA certificate (Project Settings -> Database -> SSL
#     Configuration) and pass it as ssl: { ca } to PrismaPg in
#     prisma.config.ts and src/lib/db.ts. Keeps verification.
#   - Fall back to `sslmode=no-verify`, which keeps the connection encrypted
#     but stops checking who is on the other end. On a network that already
#     intercepts TLS this changes little in practice; anywhere else it means
#     a man-in-the-middle is undetectable, so treat it as a local-development
#     workaround and not a deployment setting.

# Both host templates follow the project's own Connect -> ORMs -> Prisma panel;
# copy the real ones from there rather than assembling them. Port 6543 is the shared TRANSACTION-mode
# pooler; port 5432 is the shared SESSION-mode pooler, which is what Supabase
# itself recommends for migrations.
#
# The non-pooled direct host (db.[YOUR-PROJECT-REF].supabase.co) resolves to
# IPv6 only, so it will not work from a connection without IPv6. That is why
# both URLs above go through the pooler.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Auth (Auth.js v5 — see docs/AUTH.md)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generate your own — this file is committed, so it must never hold a real
# one. src/lib/env.ts enforces a 32-character minimum at boot.
#   openssl rand -base64 32
AUTH_SECRET=""
AUTH_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# Email + password login. Renamed from ALLOW_DEV_LOGIN, which src/lib/env.ts
# now rejects outright. It is throttled per email and per IP, so unlike the
# old provider it is fine to leave on in a real deployment.
ALLOW_PASSWORD_LOGIN="true"
ALLOW_REGISTRATION="true"

# Optional — Google sign-in. Leave empty to keep the provider unregistered.
# Google Cloud Console -> APIs & Services -> Credentials -> OAuth client ID
# (Web application), redirect URI http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback/google
AUTH_GOOGLE_ID=""
AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET=""

# Optional — magic-link email via Resend. Empty = provider unregistered.
AUTH_RESEND_KEY=""
EMAIL_FROM="journal@example.com"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Screenshot storage
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Empty S3_BUCKET = files are written to ./.uploads and served through an
# authenticated route. Good enough until you deploy somewhere with an ephemeral
# filesystem; then point this at Supabase Storage (it is S3-compatible) or any
# other S3 bucket. See src/lib/storage.ts.
S3_ENDPOINT=""
S3_REGION=""
S3_BUCKET=""
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=""
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=""
